		{"id":"636e8e8c089f4228","type":"text","text":"# [Grasp](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GRASP_(object-oriented_design)) \n**G**eneral\n**R**esponsibility\n**A**ssignment\n**S**oftware\n**P**atterns","x":-3690,"y":-3860,"width":180,"height":220},
		{"id":"438f62c0870f69df","type":"file","file":"Software Dev/Principles and Architecture/Design patterns/Structural/Flyweight.md","x":-5540,"y":-3595,"width":580,"height":285,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"54739e6a53a0078e","type":"text","text":"A fun true story regarding [[Builder]]. So i was working in .Net for a few years, and in the office i start talking with a peer that was working in Java. \n\nWe were talking about design patterns and where we've actually used them in code, and he shows me how Builders are used everywhere in Java... and i keep wondering **why**? I've never ever used them in .Net, why is Java's whole ecosystem built around them... there must be **something** they're doing right... but coming from .Net i couldn't really figure it out.\n\nWe chatted like this for about 6 months, and every time builders in Java came up i would keep challenging their need. **why Why WHY?** Why are they needed?! Why go through all this when you could just have a constructor with optional parameters?\n\nAnd that's when it hit me... that Java might not have optional parameters.","x":-6100,"y":-4200,"width":360,"height":300,"color":"5"},
